# Performance rules

The hot path is evaluation: `eval`, `eval_or`, `eval_batch_into`, and the `_par` forms. Construction may allocate.

## Allocation

- The evaluation APIs do not allocate. No `Vec::new`, `vec![]`, `clone`, `to_owned`, `collect`, `Box`, or `String` inside them or their callees. A buffer is passed in by the caller or sized at construction.
- `eval_batch` returns a `Vec` and so allocates by definition. `eval_batch_into_par` adds no allocation of its own (`rayon` internals are out of count). `eval_batch_par` allocates the result and one `Vec` for each chunk, which is what the serial `eval_batch` it calls returns (`docs/design.ja.md` §3.2).
- `tests/alloc.rs` asserts zero allocations for these APIs on every interpolator. Adding an exception needs an Issue and a design entry.
  - One exception stands (#105, `docs/design.ja.md` §3.2 and §6.3): the `neighbors` mode of `RBFInterpolator` solves a small system at every evaluation, in a working space each thread keeps. The space is allocated at the first evaluation on a thread, and widened when an interpolator with more neighbors is first evaluated there. No evaluation after that allocates, and `tests/alloc.rs` asserts that. A working space is not a way around the rule for a type that can size its buffers at construction.
- A function that allocates is named `to_*` or returns an owned value.

## Memory layout

- Data read together is stored together. Do not split into structure-of-arrays what one evaluation reads as a unit.
- `PPoly` coefficients: a flat array, interval-major, then power, then output column (`[interval][power][column]`, column innermost).
- Grid values: C order, last axis fastest, as a flat `Vec<T>` with `shape: [usize; D]`.
- Scattered points: row-major `&[f64]` (point `i` is `i*D..(i+1)*D`). Converting `[[f64; D]]` to `&[f64]` copies nothing. Do not store points column-major, transpose, or keep a second copy.
- Walk in memory order: the innermost loop runs over the contiguous axis.
- Size the data by the cache: a per-point cache goes in a row the reader already loads.

## Dispatch and bounds

- No `dyn` dispatch on the hot path. Interpolator behavior is static: generics, enums matched once per call (not per element), or const generics (`D`) that unroll.
- `unsafe` is forbidden, so remove bounds checks with `chunks_exact`, `zip`, iterators, and slices split once outside the loop. `get_unchecked` is not available.
- Interval search is a binary search or a caller-held `Hint`. A `Hint` is a value the caller owns or a batch-local; `&self` evaluation is pure (`.cursor/rules/api-contract.mdc`).
- Do not branch per element on data that is constant per call; hoist it.
- `#[inline]` and `#[inline(always)]` only where a measurement shows an effect.

## Dependency chains

A loop whose every step needs the result of the step before it (a running sum, a forward or back substitution) runs at the speed of that chain, not of its count of operations. Fewer operations on a longer chain is slower.

- Keep the chain between two steps short, and compute what does not depend on the last step beside it.
- Give independent work its own chain: sum each level of a nested sum on its own, and solve several right-hand sides of one system together, value `i` of every one side by side, so that one step works on all of them.
- Measure both forms. Three times in M1 and M2 the form with fewer operations lost (the tridiagonal sweep, partial products carried between the corners of a cell, one pass that checks two directions).

## SIMD and parallelism

- Explicit SIMD is not in v0.1.0 unless a measurement shows a bottleneck. If added, it uses a safe crate (`wide` or similar), and its result is bit for bit the scalar path's result. FMA is used only when the error bound includes it.
- A type has two batch methods, `eval_batch` and `eval_batch_into`. When one is overridden for speed, read where the other goes, and send both through one path. In #116 only `eval_batch_into` was overridden, and the PR body said both took the same path without that having been read.
- `rayon` appears only in the outermost `eval_batch_*_par`. No nested `par_iter` inside a parallel region. Whether a batch is shared out, and in chunks of what length, is decided from the time its first points take, not from a fixed number of points: no fixed chunk fits an evaluation of 5 ns and one of 5 µs (`docs/design.ja.md` §3.2 has the measurements).
- `eval_batch_par` and `eval_batch_into_par` return results bit for bit equal to the serial forms: each chunk is a call of the serial batch, whose results are those of `eval` whatever the chunk. A test compares them, with one thread and with several.
- A table the construction sizes from the data (cells, buckets, a grid of starts) is bounded by the size of the input in every dimension, and measured at a high dimension as well as at 2 and 3. `LinearNDInterpolator` cut its box into `ceil(n^(1/D))` cells an axis: at least 2, so `2^D` cells for a few points, a million at 20 dimensions for 62 simplices, and an overflow of the count at 64 (the review of #183). The largest `p` with `p^D` no more than `n` is the bound now.

## Claims

A speed or memory improvement is claimed with numbers against a named baseline (`.cursor/rules/bench.mdc`). Not with a hunch, an instruction count, or a micro-benchmark of a different path.
